Method of solving this CAT Question from Algebra

Given that x + 1 = x ^{2} and x > 0 then we have to find the value of 2x ^{4} . This 2x ^{4} can be found by either finding x first so let us take, ⟹ x + 1 = x ^{2} ⟹ x ^{2} - x – 1 = 0 ⟹ x ^{2} - x + \(\frac{1}{4}\) = 1 + \(\frac{1}{4}\) ⟹ (x - \(\frac{1}{2}\) ^{2} = \(\frac{5}{4}\) ⟹ x - \(\frac{1}{2}\) = ± \(\frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}\) ⟹ x = \(\frac{1}{2}\) ± \(\frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}\) ⟹ x + 1 = x ^{2} ⟹ (x + 1) ^{2} = (x ^{2} ) ^{2} ⟹ x ^{2} + 2x + 1 = x ^{4} ⟹ x + 1 + 2x + 1 = x ^{4} ⟹ 3x + 2 = x ^{4} ⟹ 6x + 4 = 2x ^{4} We have already found the value of x = \(\frac{1}{2}\) ± \(\frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}\), so we can substitute that in place of x. We will get, ⟹ 6(\(\frac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}\)) + 4 = 2x ^{4} ⟹ 3 + 3\sqrt{5} + 4 = 2x ^{4} ⟹ 7 + 3\sqrt{5} = 2x ^{4} Hence if x + 1 = x ^{2} and x > 0, then 2x ^{4} is equal to 7 + 3\sqrt{5}. Key thing here is to somehow find x, and then write x ^{4} in terms of x and then find the value of 2x ^{4}

The question is "If x + 1 = x ^{2} and x > 0, then 2x ^{4} is :"

Hence, the answer is 7 + 3\sqrt{5}

